I’ll inspect the specimen metadata, pattern, frozen index, and the learned analysis tooling first. Then I’ll identify coherent phase families, build and refine a GSAS-II model, verify replay stability, run the required bounded polish once, and export all deliverables from the accepted project.

The chemistry is the 1:1 WO₃ + Mn₃O₄ reaction fired at 800 °C, so the ordinary first-pass candidates are manganese tungstate plus residual/decomposition or off-stoichiometric Mn–W–O phases. I’m now checking the frozen-library interface, scorer consistency requirements, and the exact polish constraints before touching a project.

Peak-family matching supports a two-phase hypothesis: wolframite MnWO₄ explains the dominant family, while the coherent residual family (notably the ~20.3/20.9° pair and linked peaks near 27, 32–33, 36, 39–40, 46–47, and 55°) is consistent with Mn₂WO₆. I’m preserving the two serious Mn₂WO₆ topology basins—orthorhombic Pbcn (with Fe₂WO₆-derived starts) and tetragonal trirutile P4₂/mnm (Cr/V-derived starts)—for equal refinement comparisons.

The equally developed comparison selects the ordered orthorhombic Pbcn basin. It remains positive and explains a linked reflection family (~11–18 wt% at these early checkpoints), whereas the viable trirutile start contributes only ~2–4 wt% and leaves a worse fit; its Cr-derived sister start failed numerically, which I treat as unknown rather than evidence. The independent V-derived physical start is sufficient to reject that topology. I’m now finishing the accepted Pbcn model and checking whether any coherent residual family remains.

The accepted model’s scale/cell checkpoint is stable at ~89.6/10.4 wt% before profile terms. Coordinate shifts attempted to run to their bounds, so those changes are not yet trustworthy and will be rolled back or stabilized. Adding peak-width physics immediately reduces Rwp from ~37.5% to ~25.8%; I’m continuing with phase-specific broadening so the global instrument terms are not left pinned as surrogates.

A second ordinary residual family is now clear: peaks at ~23.5, 26.9, 29.1, 33.7–35.6, 46.2, and the dense 53–56° group match residual monoclinic WO₃ much better than Mn₃O₄. This also restores reaction balance with Mn₂WO₆ (Mn₂WO₆ + WO₃ has the starting 1:1 Mn:W ratio). The Mn₃O₄ test remained marginal (~1 wt%) and did not materially improve the fit, so I’m testing symmetry-diverse WO₃ references next.

The three-phase inventory now closes the obvious peak families: MnWO₄ + monoclinic WO₃ + ordered Pbcn Mn₂WO₆. The remaining fit limitation is profile modeling, not a coherent new set of Bragg positions. Isotropic broadening drives Mn₂WO₆ to tested bounds and destabilizes its fraction, so I’m rejecting those pinned branches and testing the supplied uniaxial microstrain axes with the global instrument profile held at interior values.

The unassigned family has indexed cleanly: substituting W for Te in the published R‑3 Mn₃TeO₆ framework predicts the observed doublet and all major linked peaks (20.3/20.9, 29.1, 32.3, 35.6, 46–47, 54.9–57.0, and 67.0°). The reaction-balanced phase is Mn₃WO₆, paired with residual WO₃. I’m now comparing R‑3 starts against the serious Pnma and C2/c alternatives under identical cell/coordinate adaptation; the earlier Mn₂WO₆ model will be discarded if this closes the profile physically.

The dominant wolframite and residual WO₃ are secure; the remaining coherent reflection family matches rhombohedral Mn₃WO₆. I’m now comparing symmetry-diverse Mn₃WO₆ starts on equal refinement stages, then I’ll build and finish a clean three-phase GSAS-II model before the one permitted bounded polish.

The frozen-library screen has completed and independently ranks Mn₃TeO₆-type \(R\bar3\) first; Cd₃TeO₆ gives a second physical start in the same topology. Orthorhombic and monoclinic alternatives rank substantially lower, but I’ll still give each the same cell/coordinate development before discarding them.

The first rhombohedral trial exposed an important refinement safeguard: the cell and scale settle cleanly, but unconstrained minor-phase coordinates are not supported by the data and collapse onto special positions when forced. I’m changing the comparison harness to audit and roll back any frozen, worse-fitting, or short-contact coordinate branch; topology selection will use the best physical checkpoint, not a numerically lower but chemically invalid one.

Equal-checkpoint results are separating cleanly: both \(R\bar3\) starts converge to the same basin, while C2/c and Pnma either fit worse or require unphysical coordinate motion. Their coordinate “improvements” are being explicitly rejected and rolled back. The remaining lower-ranked R3/cubic controls are running now.

The structural checkpoint is now physical and replay-oriented: zero offset was rejected because it was 99.99% correlated with specimen displacement and pushed displacement to a bound. Only the dominant MnWO₄ cation coordinates showed a small, stable supported shift (0.039 Å RMS); all other coordinate branches exceeded the known reference-mismatch scale or produced short contacts and were rolled back. I’m moving on to phase-specific peak-shape development from that checkpoint.

The profile work has revealed that the “remaining family” is actually two coherent subsets, not one broadened phase: the accepted \(R\bar3\) model accounts for the 32.26°, 35.62°, 46.20°, 54.9° and 66.96° lines, while sharp positive residuals at 20.88°, 29.15°, 47.02°, 55.85° and 57.00° remain at incompatible positions. I’m pausing nuisance refinement and remapping that second subset against the previously tested ordered Mn–W oxide candidates; treating these as mere peak-width errors would be physically wrong.

The lower-symmetry C2/c basin has now decisively closed the split residual family. Once the same accepted MnWO₄ coordinates and fully settled cells are used, its Rwp falls from 15.7% to 11.0% with no frozen variables, and the phase fraction rises to a chemically coherent ~16.8 wt% (WO₃ ~14.5 wt%). This is the first checkpoint that explains both peak subsets without boundary-broadened smearing; \(R\bar3\) is therefore being discarded. I’m now line-searching only small, geometry-checked C2/c coordinate adaptations.

The C2/c Mn₃WO₆ model has closed the remaining peak families and is already clearly better than the competing physical starts. I’m completing the equal coordinate-adaptation checkpoints now, then I’ll finish only supported profile/specimen terms and retest whether any fourth phase survives in that closed model.

The equal-start comparison remains stable: after the same two heavy-atom adaptation passes, the Zn-derived C2/c basin is at about 10.04% Rwp while the Co-derived basin is still above 14%. Both remain geometrically physical; I’m letting the second start finish so the rejection is based on matched development, not an early checkpoint.

The coordinate search confirms the Zn-derived topology, but its second pass starts exploiting unmodeled peak-shape/intensity effects: it asks for ~0.11 Å RMS heavy-atom motion, beyond the supplied reference-mismatch scale. I’m therefore developing the conservative checkpoint (~0.034 Å RMS, 0.08 Å maximum) through the full profile model; this keeps the structural comparison decisive without baking profile error into atomic positions.

The conservative model is now in the phase-specific profile pass. The ordinary MnWO₄ and WO₃ broadening terms are already stable; the remaining long step is the C2/c phase, whose center/wing residuals make its crystallite-size/strain refinement the important profile test.

The center/wing mismatch was resolved without moving the structure: Mn₃WO₆ refined to zero microstrain and a 0.131 µm domain size, lowering Rwp from 10.75% to 8.21%. The remaining C2/c residuals alternate by reflection family, so I’m testing directional broadening and March–Dollase intensity effects rather than adding another phase.

The joint replay exposed one unsupported term: MnWO₄ Uiso ran through its lower bound when polarization and broadening were released together. I’m rolling that Uiso branch back and repeating the same terminal settle; the other accepted terms remained interior, with no near-singular correlations.

The three-phase model is now terminal: all retained terms are interior and replay-stable, the background stays positive, and two Pbcn Mn₂WO₆ starts contribute only 0.26–0.43 wt% with no coherent family (Mn₃O₄ and trirutile scales collapse to zero). I’m freezing this checkpoint as `final.gpx` and invoking the required bounded polish once; after that I will only audit and export.

The one-shot polish completed cleanly. It selected the heavy-then-oxygen branch at 7.259% Rwp (baseline 7.506%); the branch converged after rolling bound-hit coordinates back, retained stable fractions, and passed the polish geometry/fraction audit. I’m now doing read-only geometry/replay checks and exporting from that exact selected project.

A read-only GSAS diagnostic reserialized the copied project (the numerical model was unchanged, but its file hash changed). I caught that consistency issue: I’m restoring `final.gpx` byte-for-byte from the accepted `polished.gpx`, then rerunning the zero-optimization replay and all exports so every submitted artifact matches the exact accepted project.

Completed and verified by zero-optimization replay of `final.gpx`.

